OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026", "title": "A strict inverse-first-power bound for univalent functions", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "072", "family_title": "Brennan's conjecture and the integral-means spectrum", "abstract_html": "We prove a uniform upper bound for inverse-first-power integral means of normalized univalent disk maps with exponent strictly below 1/4. Consequently, the bounded universal integral-means spectrum satisfies \\(B_b(-1)\\lt 1/4\\), disproving Kraetzer's conjectured spectrum at <i>p</i> = −1.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist constants \\(0<\\varepsilon<1/4\\) and \\(C<\\infty\\), independent of \\(f\\), such that \\( M_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C(1-r)^{-1/4+\\varepsilon} \\qquad \\left(f\\in\\mathcal S,\\ \\frac12\\le r<1\\right). \\) Consequently …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform inverse-first-power bound (original Theorem 1.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb D=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|&lt;1\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S\\) consist of the injective holomorphic maps \\(f:\\mathbb D\\to\\mathbb C\\) satisfying \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f'(0)=1\\), and let \\(\\mathcal S_b\\subset\\mathcal S\\) consist of those maps with bounded image.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(p\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(0\\le r&lt;1\\), define\n  \\[\n  M_p[f'](r)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}|f'(re^{i\\theta})|^p\\,d\\theta,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\beta_f(p)=\\limsup_{r\\uparrow1}\n  \\frac{\\log M_p[f'](r)}{\\log(1/(1-r))},\n  \\qquad\n  B_b(p)=\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal S_b}\\beta_f(p).\n  \\]\n  A univalent map has nonvanishing derivative, so every real power above is defined.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist constants \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;1/4\\) and \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), independent of \\(f\\), such that\n\\[\nM_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C(1-r)^{-1/4+\\varepsilon}\n\\qquad\n\\left(f\\in\\mathcal S,\\ \\frac12\\le r&lt;1\\right).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nB_b(-1)&lt;\\frac14,\n\\]\nand Kraetzer’s conjectured identity\n\\[\nB_b(p)=\n\\begin{cases}\np^2/4,&amp; |p|\\le2,\\\\\n|p|-1,&amp; |p|\\ge2\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nis false.</p><p>Both constants are independent of the image size. The argument establishes existence of a positive gap without evaluating it numerically.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\beta\\) be the single operator-growth exponent from Proposition 1. Suppose, toward a contradiction, that \\(\\beta\\ge1/4\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 constructs the probability law \\(P\\). By Proposition 4,\n\\[\nk^2\\mathbb EJ_F(i)\n=\\frac{(4\\beta-1)(\\beta+2)}9,\n\\qquad\nk=\\frac{\\beta+2}{3}.\n\\]\nProposition 6 gives \\(\\mathbb EJ_F(i)\\le0\\). These identities force\n\\[\n\\beta=\\frac14,\n\\qquad\nk=\\frac34,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb EJ_F(i)=0.\n\\]\nThe equality assertion of Proposition 6 then gives \\(J_F(i)=0\\) almost surely.</p><p>Apply (10) to the test \\(|J_F(i)|\\). By the rebasing identity (21) and the strictly positive weight \\(|Q_F(z)|\\), one has \\(J_F(z)=0\\) almost surely at every fixed \\(z\\in\\mathbb H\\). A countable dense set and continuity give \\(J_F\\equiv0\\) for almost every \\(F\\), contradicting Proposition 7. Hence\n\\[\n\\beta&lt;\\frac14.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(b\\) with\n\\[\n\\beta&lt;b&lt;\\frac14.\n\\]\nThe root limit in Proposition 1 supplies a constant \\(C_b\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|L^n\\|\\le C_b2^{bn}\n\\]\nfor all \\(n\\). For\n\\[\n2^{-n}\\le1-r&lt;2^{1-n},\n\\]\nthe disk comparison in Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\nM_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C(1-r)^{-b},\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(f\\in\\mathcal S\\) and \\(1/2\\le r&lt;1\\).</p><p>Taking \\(\\varepsilon=1/4-b\\) proves (2). In particular,\n\\[\nB_b(-1)\\le b&lt;\\frac14,\n\\]\nwhereas the conjectured formula (1) gives \\(B_b(-1)=1/4\\). This proves the stated disproof.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The space \\(\\mathcal K\\) is compact metrizable, the operators \\(A_z\\) and \\(L\\) are bounded and positive, and \\( \\rho=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|L^n\\|^{1/n}, \\qquad \\beta=\\log_2\\rho \\) exist, with \\(0\\le\\beta\\le1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Compact half-plane model, operator growth, and disk comparison (Section 2 and original Proposition 2.3).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb H=\\{x+iy:y&gt;0\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal K=\\{F:\\mathbb H\\to\\mathbb C\\text{ univalent}:F(i)=0,\\ F'(i)=1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Give \\(\\mathcal K\\) the locally uniform topology, and put \\(Q_F=1/F'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  m(z)=\\frac{z-i}{z+i}.\n  \\]\n  The maps in \\(\\mathcal K\\) are exactly \\(F=2if\\circ m\\), \\(f\\in\\mathcal S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Identify \\(z=x+iy\\in\\mathbb H\\) with the affine map\n  \\[\n  z\\cdot w=x+yw,\n  \\qquad\n  z^\\#=\\frac{-x+i}{y}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z=x+iy\\), set\n  \\[\n  (T_zF)(w)=\\frac{F(z\\cdot w)-F(z)}{yF'(z)},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  (A_z\\Phi)(F)=|Q_F(z)|\\Phi(T_zF),\n  \\qquad\n  L=\\frac12\\left(A_{-1/2+i/2}+A_{1/2+i/2}\\right)\n  \\]\n  on \\(C(\\mathcal K)\\), with its supremum norm.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space \\(\\mathcal K\\) is compact metrizable, the operators \\(A_z\\) and \\(L\\) are bounded and positive, and\n\\[\n\\rho=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\|L^n\\|^{1/n},\n\\qquad\n\\beta=\\log_2\\rho\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nexist, with \\(0\\le\\beta\\le1\\).</p><p>For \\(v=2^{-n}\\) and \\(u_j=-1+(2j+1)v\\),\n\\[\nL^n=v\\sum_{j=0}^{2^n-1}A_{u_j+iv},\n\\qquad\n\\|L^n\\|=\\max_{F\\in\\mathcal K}(L^n1)(F).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]</p><p>There is a universal constant \\(C_0\\) such that\n\\[\nM_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C_0\\|L^n\\|\n\\]\nwhenever\n\\[\nf\\in\\mathcal S,\\qquad \\frac12\\le r&lt;1,\\qquad\n2^{-n}\\le1-r&lt;2^{1-n}.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(B_b(-1)\\le\\beta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.2 proves compactness and the continuity needed to define the operators on \\(C(\\mathcal K)\\). Lemma 1.3 proves the affine composition law, dyadic formula, norm identity, and existence and range of \\(\\beta\\). Lemma 1.4 supplies the uniform comparison with disk means and its spectral consequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(f\\in\\mathcal S\\) and \\(|z|=r<1\\), \\( \\frac{1-r}{(1+r)^3}\\le |f'(z)| \\le\\frac{1+r}{(1-r)^3}, \\qquad B(0,1/4)\\subset f(\\mathbb D). \\) If \\(F\\) is univalent on the Euclidean disk \\(B(z_0,s)\\), then …", "statement": "<p>**Classical univalence estimates (original Lemma 2.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S\\) be the normalized univalent disk class in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(f\\in\\mathcal S\\) and \\(|z|=r&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{1-r}{(1+r)^3}\\le |f'(z)|\n\\le\\frac{1+r}{(1-r)^3},\n\\qquad\nB(0,1/4)\\subset f(\\mathbb D).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nIf \\(F\\) is univalent on the Euclidean disk \\(B(z_0,s)\\), then\n\\[\nB\\!\\left(F(z_0),\\frac{s|F'(z_0)|}{4}\\right)\n\\subset F(B(z_0,s)).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The first assertions are the classical quarter and derivative-distortion theorems for normalized univalent disk maps [Dur83, Chapter 2]. Apply the quarter theorem to\n\\[\n\\frac{F(z_0+s\\zeta)-F(z_0)}{sF'(z_0)}\n\\]\nto obtain the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The locally uniform topology makes \\(\\mathcal K\\) compact metrizable.", "statement": "<p>**Compact normalized half-plane family (original Lemma 2.2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal K\\), \\(Q_F\\), and \\(m\\) from Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The locally uniform topology makes \\(\\mathcal K\\) compact metrizable. Evaluation of \\(F,Q_F\\), and all their derivatives is jointly continuous in \\(F,z\\), and uniformly bounded on each compact source set.</p><p>Moreover, uniformly in \\(F\\in\\mathcal K\\),\n\\[\n|Q_F(x+iy)|\\le \\frac Cy\n\\qquad (|x|\\le2,\\ 0&lt;y\\le1),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|F(z)|\\ge c&gt;0\n\\qquad (|m(z)|\\ge1/2).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Integrating the derivative bound in Lemma 1.1 gives local uniform boundedness in the disk. Montel’s theorem gives convergent subsequences. Limits retain the normalization by convergence of derivatives and remain injective by Hurwitz’s theorem: for a fixed source point, subtract its value and apply Hurwitz on the punctured domain. The nonconstant normalization excludes the identically zero alternative.</p><p>A countable compact exhaustion metrizes the locally uniform topology. Interior holomorphic convergence, together with nonvanishing derivatives, gives the asserted continuity and bounds.</p><p>For \\(F=2if\\circ m\\),\n\\[\n1-|m(z)|^2=\\frac{4y}{|z+i|^2},\n\\qquad\n|Q_F(z)|=\\frac{|z+i|^2}{4|f'(m(z))|}.\n\\]\nThese identities and (3) imply the strip estimate. By (4), the image under \\(f\\) of the disk of radius \\(1/2\\) contains \\(B(0,1/8)\\). Injectivity gives the second assertion, with \\(c=1/4\\) for \\(F=2if\\circ m\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(z,w\\in\\mathbb H\\), \\( T_w(T_zF)=T_{z\\cdot w}F, \\qquad Q_{T_zF}(w)=\\frac{Q_F(z\\cdot w)}{Q_F(z)}, \\) and \\( A_zA_w=A_{z\\cdot w}. \\) For each \\(\\Phi\\in C(\\mathcal K)\\), \\(A_z\\Phi\\) is jointly continuous in \\(F,z\\).", "statement": "<p>**Affine composition, dyadic sampling, and the growth exponent.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the affine action, rebasings, and operators of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(z,w\\in\\mathbb H\\),\n\\[\nT_w(T_zF)=T_{z\\cdot w}F,\n\\qquad\nQ_{T_zF}(w)=\\frac{Q_F(z\\cdot w)}{Q_F(z)},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nA_zA_w=A_{z\\cdot w}.\n\\]\nFor each \\(\\Phi\\in C(\\mathcal K)\\), \\(A_z\\Phi\\) is jointly continuous in \\(F,z\\). The dyadic identities (7) hold, and\n\\[\n1\\le\\|L^n\\|\\le C2^n.\n\\]\nThe limit (8) exists and satisfies \\(0\\le\\beta\\le1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The affine maps form a group with identity \\(i\\) and inverse \\(z^\\#\\). Direct substitution gives (6), and then \\(A_zA_w=A_{z\\cdot w}\\).</p><p>Lemma 1.2 gives boundedness and positivity of \\(A_z\\), together with joint continuity of \\(A_z\\Phi\\). Rebasings send compact source sets to compact sets locally in the parameter \\(z\\).</p><p>Iterating the two affine subscales gives\n\\[\nL^n=v\\sum_{j=0}^{2^n-1}A_{u_j+iv},\n\\qquad\nv=2^{-n},\\qquad u_j=-1+(2j+1)v.\n\\]\nThe abscissae are the sums\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n\\pm2^{-\\ell}.\n\\]\nPositivity proves\n\\[\n\\|L^n\\|=\\max_{F\\in\\mathcal K}(L^n1)(F).\n\\]</p><p>Testing the affine map \\(F(z)=z-i\\), and using (5), gives\n\\[\n1\\le\\|L^n\\|\\le C2^n.\n\\]\nSubmultiplicativity defines the root limit. Indeed, writing \\(n=qN+r\\), \\(0\\le r&lt;N\\), bounds the upper root limit by \\(\\|L^N\\|^{1/N}\\); take the infimum over \\(N\\). The displayed bounds give \\(0\\le\\beta\\le1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a universal constant \\(C_0\\) such that \\( M_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C_0\\|L^n\\|. \\) Consequently \\(B_b(-1)\\le\\beta\\).", "statement": "<p>**From dyadic samples to disk means (original Proposition 2.3).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\in\\mathcal S\\), \\(F=2if\\circ m\\), and let \\(L\\) be as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\frac12\\le r&lt;1,\\qquad\n  v=2^{-n}\\le1-r&lt;2v,\n  \\qquad\n  u_j=-1+(2j+1)v.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a universal constant \\(C_0\\) such that\n\\[\nM_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C_0\\|L^n\\|.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(B_b(-1)\\le\\beta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(3\\pi/4\\le\\theta\\le5\\pi/4\\), write\n\\[\nm^{-1}(re^{i\\theta})=x+iy,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nx=\\frac{-2r\\sin\\theta}{d},\n\\qquad\ny=\\frac{1-r^2}{d},\n\\qquad\nd=1-2r\\cos\\theta+r^2.\n\\]\nHere \\(|x|\\le1\\), \\(1/2\\le y/v\\le4\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{d\\theta}\n=\\frac{4r^2-2r(1+r^2)\\cos\\theta}{d^2}\n\\ge\\frac1{16}.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(|x-u_j|\\le v\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{x-u_j}{v}+i\\frac yv\n\\]\nlies in a fixed compact rectangle in \\(\\mathbb H\\). Apply Lemma 1.2 to \\(Q_{T_{u_j+iv}F}\\) and use (6) to obtain\n\\[\n|Q_F(x+iy)|\\le C|Q_F(u_j+iv)|.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n|f'(re^{i\\theta})|^{-1}\n=\\frac{4|Q_F(x+iy)|}{|x+i(y+1)|^2}.\n\\]\nThe denominator is bounded below. Integrating over the cells bounds the integral on this arc by\n\\[\nCv\\sum_j|Q_F(u_j+iv)|\\le C\\|L^n\\|.\n\\]</p><p>Four arcs obtained by applying the same argument to\n\\[\ne^{-ia}f(e^{ia}\\zeta)\n\\]\ncover the circle. All constants are independent of \\(f\\).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{\\log(1/(1-r))}\\longrightarrow\\frac1{\\log2}.\n\\]\nUse (8), take each map’s limit superior, and then take the supremum over \\(\\mathcal S_b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a Borel probability \\(P\\) on \\(\\mathcal K\\), with expectation denoted by \\(\\mathbb E\\), such that \\( \\mathbb E\\!\\left[ |Q_F(x+iy)|\\Phi(T_{x+iy}F) \\right] =y^{-\\beta}\\mathbb E\\Phi(F) \\) for every …", "statement": "<p>**Exact-parameter weighted affine law and logarithmic-derivative moments (original Proposition 3.1 and Lemma 3.2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal K,Q_F,T_z,A_z,L,\\rho,\\beta\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\beta=\\log_2\\rho\\ge\\frac14.\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z=x+iy\\), define\n  \\[\n  q_F(z)=iy\\frac{Q_F'(z)}{Q_F(z)}\n  =-iy\\frac{F''(z)}{F'(z)}\n  =a_F(z)+ib_F(z),\n  \\qquad\n  k=\\frac{\\beta+2}{3}.\n  \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(3(k-1)=\\beta-1\\) and \\(3/4\\le k\\le1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a Borel probability \\(P\\) on \\(\\mathcal K\\), with expectation denoted by \\(\\mathbb E\\), such that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n|Q_F(x+iy)|\\Phi(T_{x+iy}F)\n\\right]\n=y^{-\\beta}\\mathbb E\\Phi(F)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nfor every \\(x+iy\\in\\mathbb H\\) and every nonnegative Borel test \\(\\Phi:\\mathcal K\\to[0,\\infty]\\).</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\nq_{T_zF}(w)=q_F(z\\cdot w),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E a_F(i)=-\\beta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E|q_F(i)|^2=\\beta(\\beta+1).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 constructs an eigenmeasure at the exact parameter \\(\\rho\\). Lemma 2.2 turns it into the probability law \\(P\\) satisfying the weighted affine identity (10). Lemma 2.3 supplies the rebasing identity for \\(q_F\\) and the moment identities (12).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a Borel probability \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\mathcal K\\) satisfying \\( \\mu L=\\rho\\mu, \\) where measures act on the left as integration functionals.", "statement": "<p>**An operator eigenmeasure at the exact growth parameter.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the compact space \\(\\mathcal K\\) and positive operator \\(L\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\rho=\\lim_n\\|L^n\\|^{1/n}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a Borel probability \\(\\mu\\) on \\(\\mathcal K\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\mu L=\\rho\\mu,\n\\]\nwhere measures act on the left as integration functionals.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the representation of positive functionals on continuous functions of a compact metric space by Borel measures [Fol99, Theorem 7.2]. Compactness of probabilities follows by taking a diagonal limit on a countable dense family of continuous tests and applying this representation theorem.</p><p>For \\(\\tau&gt;\\rho\\), the series\n\\[\nR_\\tau=\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\tau^{-n}L^n\n\\]\nconverges in operator norm. Put\n\\[\ng_\\tau=R_\\tau1,\n\\qquad\nM_\\tau=\\max g_\\tau.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\nLg_\\tau=\\tau(g_\\tau-1)\n\\le\\tau(1-1/M_\\tau)g_\\tau,\n\\qquad\n1\\le g_\\tau\\le M_\\tau,\n\\]\npositivity gives\n\\[\n\\|L^n\\|\n\\le M_\\tau[\\tau(1-1/M_\\tau)]^n.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\rho\\le\\tau(1-1/M_\\tau),\n\\qquad\nM_\\tau\\ge\\frac{\\tau}{\\tau-\\rho}\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>At a maximizer \\(F_\\tau\\) of \\(g_\\tau\\), the functional\n\\[\n\\mu_\\tau=\\frac{\\delta_{F_\\tau}R_\\tau}{M_\\tau}\n\\]\nis a probability, and\n\\[\n\\mu_\\tau L\n=\\tau\\mu_\\tau-\\frac{\\tau}{M_\\tau}\\delta_{F_\\tau}.\n\\]\nA weak limit as \\(\\tau\\downarrow\\rho\\) gives the required \\(\\mu\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The measure \\(\\mu\\) satisfies \\(\\mu A_{2+i}=\\mu\\).", "statement": "<p>**Horizontal and scale averages of the eigenmeasure.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu\\) be the probability from Lemma 2.1, so \\(\\mu L=\\rho\\mu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In particular \\(\\beta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The measure \\(\\mu\\) satisfies \\(\\mu A_{2+i}=\\mu\\). Horizontal averaging and then multiplicative scale averaging produce a Borel probability \\(P\\) satisfying (10).</p>", "proof": "<p>The composition law gives\n\\[\nA_{u+iv}A_{2+i}=A_{u+2v+iv}.\n\\]\nRight translation by \\(2\\) shifts the grid in (7) by one cell of width \\(2v\\). Using \\(\\mu=\\rho^{-n}\\mu L^n\\), all but the two endpoint terms cancel:\n\\[\n\\mu A_{2+i}-\\mu\n=\\rho^{-n}v\n\\left(\\mu A_{1+v+iv}-\\mu A_{-1+v+iv}\\right).\n\\]\nThe norm of this difference is at most \\(2C\\rho^{-n}\\) by (5), and tends to zero because \\(\\beta&gt;0\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\mu A_{2+i}=\\mu.\n\\]\nIt follows that \\(t\\mapsto\\mu A_{t+ih}\\) has period two for every fixed \\(h&gt;0\\).</p><p>Define, on continuous tests,\n\\[\n\\bar\\mu=\\frac12\\int_0^2\\mu A_{x+i}\\,dx.\n\\]\nPeriodicity gives\n\\[\n\\bar\\mu A_{s+i}=\\bar\\mu\n\\qquad(s\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\rho\\bar\\mu\n=\\frac12\\int_0^2\\mu LA_{x+i}\\,dx\n=\\frac12\\int_{-1/2}^{3/2}\\mu A_{t+i/2}\\,dt\n=\\bar\\mu A_{i/2}.\n\\]\nThe middle equality uses \\(t=-1/2+x/2\\) and \\(t=1/2+x/2\\) for the two summands of \\(L\\); the last uses period two.</p><p>Consequently \\(h^\\beta\\bar\\mu A_{ih}\\) is multiplicatively two-periodic in \\(h&gt;0\\). Each such functional is horizontally invariant on the right, because\n\\[\nA_{ih}A_{s+i}=A_{hs+i}A_{ih}.\n\\]\nThe functional\n\\[\n\\nu=\\int_1^2 h^\\beta\\bar\\mu A_{ih}\\,\\frac{dh}{h}\n\\]\nhas finite, strictly positive mass by compact-family continuity. Periodicity and the substitution \\(u=hy\\) give\n\\[\n\\nu A_{iy}=y^{-\\beta}\\nu.\n\\]\nHorizontal invariance persists, so normalization of \\(\\nu\\) gives (10) for continuous tests.</p><p>For each fixed \\(x+iy\\), the two sides of (10) define finite Borel measures, one being a weighted pushforward. Equality on continuous tests makes them equal as measures, proving (10) for all nonnegative Borel tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The rebasing identity \\( q_{T_zF}(w)=q_F(z\\cdot w) \\) holds, and \\( \\mathbb E a_F(i)=-\\beta, \\qquad \\mathbb E|q_F(i)|^2=\\beta(\\beta+1). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Logarithmic-derivative moments (original Lemma 3.2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) satisfy (10).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(q_F=a_F+ib_F\\) as in (11).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rebasing identity\n\\[\nq_{T_zF}(w)=q_F(z\\cdot w)\n\\]\nholds, and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E a_F(i)=-\\beta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E|q_F(i)|^2=\\beta(\\beta+1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The rebasing identity follows from (6).</p><p>The constant test in (10) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|Q_F(x+iy)|=y^{-\\beta}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\log|Q_F|\\) is harmonic,\n\\[\n\\partial_y|Q_F|=\\frac{|Q_F|a_F}{y},\n\\qquad\n\\Delta|Q_F|\n=|Q_F|\\left|\\frac{Q_F'}{Q_F}\\right|^2.\n\\]\nDifferentiate at \\(i\\), where \\(Q_F(i)=1\\). Compact-family continuity and local nonvanishing give the uniform smoothness bounds needed to differentiate under expectation. Differentiating in \\(y\\) gives the first moment, and applying the Laplacian gives the second.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(P\\)-almost every \\(F\\), and then for area-almost every \\(\\xi\\in\\mathbb C\\), every closed positive superlevel \\( \\{z\\in\\mathbb H:V_\\xi^F(z)\\ge\\delta\\}, \\qquad \\delta>0, \\) is compact in \\(\\mathbb H\\).", "statement": "<p>**Compact positive superlevels (original Proposition 4.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\beta\\ge1/4\\), and let \\(P\\) be the law from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(k=(\\beta+2)/3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(F\\in\\mathcal K\\), \\(\\xi\\in\\mathbb C\\), and \\(z=x+iy\\in\\mathbb H\\), define\n  \\[\n  V_\\xi^F(x+iy)=\\frac{y^k}{|F(x+iy)-\\xi|},\n  \\]\n  with value \\(+\\infty\\) at the possible pole.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(dA\\) for planar Lebesgue area.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(P\\)-almost every \\(F\\), and then for area-almost every \\(\\xi\\in\\mathbb C\\), every closed positive superlevel\n\\[\n\\{z\\in\\mathbb H:V_\\xi^F(z)\\ge\\delta\\},\n\\qquad \\delta&gt;0,\n\\]\nis compact in \\(\\mathbb H\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(F\\) satisfying Lemma 3.1. Lemma 3.2 controls source infinity. It remains to control approach to the real boundary, uniformly in the horizontal coordinate for almost every target.</p><p>For omitted targets, meaning \\(\\xi\\notin F(\\mathbb H)\\), restrict to \\(|\\xi|\\le R\\) and fix \\(\\delta&gt;0\\). At scale \\(h=2^{-\\ell}\\), \\(\\ell\\ge0\\), partition the line into cells\n\\[\n[jh,(j+1)h).\n\\]\nMark a cell if some \\(z=x+iy\\), with \\(x\\) in that cell and \\(h\\le y\\le2h\\), satisfies\n\\[\n|F(z)-\\xi|\\le y^k/\\delta\n\\]\nfor at least one such omitted target.</p><p>At a witness, (4) on \\(B(z,y)\\) gives\n\\[\ny|F'(z)|\\le4|F(z)-\\xi|.\n\\]\nFor every \\(x'\\) in the same cell, the rebased point\n\\[\n\\frac{x'-x}{y}+i\\frac hy\n\\]\nbelongs to the compact rectangle \\([-1,1]+i[1/2,1]\\). Compact-family bounds applied to \\(T_zF\\) therefore give\n\\[\n|F(x'+ih)-\\xi|\\le C_\\delta h^k.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\nThis bound holds for every witness and its target.</p><p>Each marked cell’s entire segment at height \\(h\\) is mapped into\n\\[\n\\{|\\omega|\\le R+C_\\delta\\}.\n\\]\nEquation (15) bounds the number of marked cells by \\(C/h\\). Disks of radius \\(C_\\delta h^k\\), centered at their midpoint images, cover all targets witnessed at this scale and have summed area \\(O(h^{2k-1})\\).</p><p>The sum over dyadic scales is finite because \\(k&gt;1/2\\). The limsup of these measurable covering unions has area zero. Taking integer \\(R&gt;0\\) and \\(\\delta=1/n\\), \\(n\\ge1\\), shows that for almost every omitted target,\n\\[\n\\sup_xV_\\xi^F(x+iy)\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad(y\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nIndeed, a failure at arbitrarily small heights gives witnesses in infinitely many dyadic bands for one such threshold.</p><p>If \\(\\xi\\in F(\\mathbb H)\\), a small closed disk about \\(\\xi\\), contained in \\(F(\\mathbb H)\\), has compact inverse image under the homeomorphism onto the image. Below its minimum preimage height, \\(|F(z)-\\xi|\\) is uniformly positive, giving the same limit.</p><p>At heights bounded below, (14) gives \\(|F(z)|\\to\\infty\\) and \\(V_\\xi^F(z)\\to0\\) at source infinity. Finally,\n\\[\n\\{V_\\xi^F\\ge\\delta\\}\n=\\{|F(z)-\\xi|\\le y^k/\\delta\\}\n\\]\nis relatively closed, also at a pole. The two escape exclusions prove compactness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(P\\)-almost every \\(F\\), \\( I(F):=\\int_{S_0}y^\\beta|F(z)|^{-3}\\,dA(z)<\\infty. \\)", "statement": "<p>**The weighted area tail (original Lemma 4.2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\beta,P,m\\) from Propositions 1 and 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  S_0=\\{z\\in\\mathbb H:|m(z)|\\ge1/2\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(P\\)-almost every \\(F\\),\n\\[\nI(F):=\\int_{S_0}y^\\beta|F(z)|^{-3}\\,dA(z)&lt;\\infty.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(z\\ne i\\), apply (10) to\n\\[\n\\Phi(F)=|F(z^\\#)|^{-3}.\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\n(T_zF)(z^\\#)=-\\frac{F(z)Q_F(z)}{y}\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[|F'(z)|^2|F(z)|^{-3}\\right]\n=y^{-\\beta-3}\\mathbb E|F(z^\\#)|^{-3}.\n\\]</p><p>The integral of the left side over \\(S_0\\) is uniformly finite: by injectivity and (5), it is bounded by\n\\[\n\\int_{|\\omega|\\ge c}|\\omega|^{-3}\\,dA(\\omega).\n\\]\nThe involution \\(z\\mapsto z^\\#\\) preserves \\(|m(z)|\\), inverts the height, and has absolute Jacobian \\(y^{-3}\\). Changing variables on the right leaves exactly the weight \\(y^\\beta\\). Tonelli proves finite expectation of \\(I\\), hence the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\( \\frac{y^k}{|F(x+iy)|}\\longrightarrow0 \\qquad\\text{as }|x|+y\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\) For every compact target set \\(K\\subset\\mathbb C\\), its preimage restricted to \\(y\\ge\\epsilon>0\\) is compact, its whole …", "statement": "<p>**Escape at infinity and horizontal length (original Lemma 4.3).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(F\\in\\mathcal K\\) satisfying \\(I(F)&lt;\\infty\\), with \\(I\\) as in Lemma 3.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(k=(\\beta+2)/3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n\\frac{y^k}{|F(x+iy)|}\\longrightarrow0\n\\qquad\\text{as }|x|+y\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nFor every compact target set \\(K\\subset\\mathbb C\\), its preimage restricted to \\(y\\ge\\epsilon&gt;0\\) is compact, its whole preimage has bounded height, and\n\\[\n\\sup_{s&gt;0}\\int_{\\mathbb R}1_K(F(x+is))\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(w=x'+iy'\\in B(z,y/2)\\), then\n\\[\ny/2\\le y'\\le3y/2,\n\\qquad\n|x|+y\\le |x'|+3y'.\n\\]\nThus these disks escape every bounded \\(|x'|+y'\\) region as their centers do. The complement of \\(S_0\\) is bounded, so the disks are eventually contained in \\(S_0\\) and avoid the zero of \\(F\\).</p><p>Submean for \\(|1/F|^3\\), together with \\(3k=\\beta+2\\), gives\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{y^k}{|F(z)|}\\right)^3\n\\le C_\\beta\\int_{B(z,y/2)}\n(\\operatorname{Im}w)^\\beta|F(w)|^{-3}\\,dA(w).\n\\]\nThe right side tends to zero as a tail of (13).</p><p>For \\(|F(z)|\\) bounded and \\(y\\ge\\epsilon\\), this excludes source infinity, proving strip compactness. It also excludes unbounded heights in the whole preimage of \\(K\\).</p><p>Choose a nonnegative smooth compactly supported \\(\\psi\\ge1_K\\), and put\n\\[\nH(y)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\psi(F(x+iy))\\,dx.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(s&gt;0\\), the support in \\(y\\ge s/2\\) is compact by the strip assertion. Hence differentiation and integration by parts give\n\\[\nH(s)=\\int_s^\\infty(y-s)H''(y)\\,dy\n=\\int_{y&gt;s}(y-s)|F'(z)|^2(\\Delta\\psi)(F(z))\\,dA(z).\n\\]\nHere the integral of \\(\\partial_{xx}(\\psi\\circ F)\\) is zero and\n\\[\n\\Delta(\\psi\\circ F)=|F'|^2(\\Delta\\psi)\\circ F.\n\\]\nIf \\(Y_\\psi\\) bounds the preimage heights of \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\psi\\), the absolute value is at most\n\\[\nY_\\psi\\int_{\\mathbb H}|F'(z)|^2|(\\Delta\\psi)(F(z))|\\,dA(z)\n\\le Y_\\psi\\int_{\\mathbb C}|\\Delta\\psi|\\,dA.\n\\]\nThe last inequality uses injectivity, and the bound is independent of \\(s\\). This proves (15).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The set \\(\\mathcal G\\) is Borel.", "statement": "<p>**Critical map, generic good targets, and rebasing (Section 5.1 and original Lemma 5.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\beta\\ge1/4\\), let \\(P\\) be the law of Proposition 2, and put \\(k=(\\beta+2)/3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(Q_F,q_F=a_F+ib_F\\), and \\(V_\\xi^F\\) from Propositions 2 and 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  G_F(z)=F(z)-\\frac{iyF'(z)}k,\n  \\qquad\n  J_F(z)=\\frac{\\det DG_F(z)}{|F'(z)|^2},\n  \\qquad z=x+iy.\n  \\tag{17}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal G\\subset\\mathcal K\\times\\mathbb C\\) consist of pairs \\((F,\\xi)\\) for which:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. every closed positive superlevel of \\(V_\\xi^F\\) is compact;</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. \\(\\xi\\) is a regular value of \\(G_F\\);</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. distinct preimages under \\(G_F\\) have distinct \\(V_\\xi^F\\)-values.\n  \n  Such pairs are called good.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(\\mathcal G\\) is Borel. For \\(P\\)-almost every \\(F\\), area-almost every \\(\\xi\\) is good.</p><p>Off the possible pole, critical points of \\(V_\\xi^F\\) are exactly the points satisfying \\(G_F(z)=\\xi\\). At a regular critical point, \\(J_F&gt;0\\) gives a nondegenerate saddle, and \\(J_F&lt;0\\) gives a nondegenerate local maximum, called a finite maximum.</p><p>The identities\n\\[\nk^2J_F\n=k(k-1)+(2k-1)a_F+|q_F|^2,\n\\qquad\nk^2\\mathbb EJ_F(i)\n=\\frac{(4\\beta-1)(\\beta+2)}9\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nhold.</p><p>For \\(t\\in\\mathbb H\\), \\(Y=\\operatorname{Im}t\\), and\n\\[\n\\eta=\\frac{\\xi-F(t)}{YF'(t)},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nG_{T_tF}(w)=\\frac{G_F(t\\cdot w)-F(t)}{YF'(t)},\n\\qquad\nJ_{T_tF}(w)=J_F(t\\cdot w),\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\\[\nV_\\eta^{T_tF}(w)\n=Y^{1-k}|F'(t)|\\,V_\\xi^F(t\\cdot w).\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nConsequently goodness corresponds in both directions under rebasing.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 establishes the critical equations, classification, and Jacobian identities. Lemma 4.2 establishes Borel goodness and target genericity.</p><p>For \\(t\\in\\mathbb H\\), \\(Y=\\operatorname{Im}t\\), and\n\\[\n\\eta=\\frac{\\xi-F(t)}{YF'(t)},\n\\]\ndirect calculation from the definition of \\(T_tF\\) gives (21) and (22). The affine source map is a homeomorphism of \\(\\mathbb H\\), and the potential is multiplied by the positive constant \\(Y^{1-k}|F'(t)|\\). The three conditions defining goodness therefore correspond in both directions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The real derivative columns of \\(G_F\\) are \\( \\frac{F'}k(k+q_F), \\qquad \\frac{iF'}k(k-1+q_F), \\) and (18) holds.", "statement": "<p>**Critical-point identities and classification.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F,\\beta,k,q_F,G_F,J_F,V_\\xi^F\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Away from a possible pole, set\n  \\[\n  \\ell=\\log V_\\xi^F,\n  \\qquad\n  r_0=\\frac{F'}{F-\\xi}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The real derivative columns of \\(G_F\\) are\n\\[\n\\frac{F'}k(k+q_F),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{iF'}k(k-1+q_F),\n\\]\nand (18) holds.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\n\\Delta\\ell=-\\frac{k}{y^2},\n\\qquad\n(\\ell_x,\\ell_y)=\n\\left(-\\operatorname{Re}r_0,\\frac ky+\\operatorname{Im}r_0\\right).\n\\]\nThus critical points are exactly \\(G_F(z)=\\xi\\), and no pole lies on this fiber. At a critical point,\n\\[\nV_\\xi^F(z)=ky^{k-1}|Q_F(z)|,\n\\qquad\nr_0'=\\frac{k(k+q_F)}{y^2},\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hess}\\ell\n=\\frac{k}{y^2}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n-(k+a_F)&amp;b_F\\\\\nb_F&amp;k-1+a_F\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\det\\operatorname{Hess}\\ell=-\\frac{k^4}{y^4}J_F.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(J_F&gt;0\\) gives a nondegenerate saddle and \\(J_F&lt;0\\) gives a nondegenerate finite maximum.</p>", "proof": "<p>Differentiate\n\\[\nG_F=F-\\frac{iyF'}k\n\\]\nto obtain the stated real derivative columns. Their determinant gives\n\\[\nk^2J_F=k(k-1)+(2k-1)a_F+|q_F|^2.\n\\]\nTaking expectation at \\(i\\), using (12), and substituting \\(k=(\\beta+2)/3\\), gives the second identity in (18).</p><p>Differentiating\n\\[\n\\ell=k\\log y-\\log|F-\\xi|\n\\]\ngives the displayed Laplacian and gradient. The critical equations say\n\\[\nr_0=-\\frac{ik}{y},\n\\]\nwhich is equivalent to \\(G_F(z)=\\xi\\). A pole is not such a preimage. Substitution gives (19).</p><p>Direct differentiation yields (20). Its trace is negative. Thus a positive \\(J_F\\) gives negative Hessian determinant and a saddle, while a negative \\(J_F\\) gives positive determinant and a finite maximum.</p><p>Finally, because \\(3(k-1)=\\beta-1\\), cubing the critical value gives\n\\[\n(V_\\xi^F(z))^3=k^3y^{\\beta-1}|Q_F(z)|^3.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Goodness is Borel in \\((F,\\xi)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Generic targets and Borel goodness (original Lemma 5.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the law \\(P\\), potential \\(V_\\xi^F\\), critical map \\(G_F\\), and set \\(\\mathcal G\\) from Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Goodness is Borel in \\((F,\\xi)\\). For \\(P\\)-almost every \\(F\\), area-almost every \\(\\xi\\in\\mathbb C\\) is good.</p>", "proof": "<p>The superlevel assertion is Proposition 3.</p><p>For any fixed \\(F\\), singular values of \\(G_F\\) have area zero. This is the plane case of Sard’s theorem [Sar42, Theorem 4.1]. The elementary estimate needed here is as follows. On a compact source box, cover singular points by grid squares of side \\(h\\). Linearize at one singular point of each square. Bounded second derivatives put its image in an \\(O(h^2)\\)-neighborhood of a segment of length \\(O(h)\\). Its area is \\(O(h^3)\\). There are \\(O(h^{-2})\\) squares, giving total area tending to zero. Exhaust the source by compact boxes.</p><p>Cover the nonsingular locus by countably many inverse-function charts. On an overlap where two branches \\(z_1(\\xi),z_2(\\xi)\\) are distinct, the target differential of their critical log values is\n\\[\nd_\\xi\\!\\left[\\log V_\\xi^F(z_j(\\xi))\\right](v)\n=\\operatorname{Re}\\frac{v}{F(z_j(\\xi))-\\xi}.\n\\]\nThe source derivative vanishes by criticality. The two linear functionals differ by injectivity of \\(F\\). Hence the difference of log values has nonzero differential, and its zero set is locally a smooth curve. Countably many chart pairs give an area-null tie set.</p><p>For measurability, use\n\\[\nC_m=\\{|x|\\le m,\\ 1/m\\le y\\le m\\}.\n\\]\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge1\\), require the open set\n\\[\n\\{V_\\xi^F&gt;1/n\\}\n=\\{|F(z)-\\xi|&lt;ny^k\\}\n\\]\nto lie in some \\(C_m\\). Failure of a given containment is detected at rational-coordinate points outside the closed box. This is a Borel test. It is equivalent to compactness of all closed positive superlevels: for a closed level choose a strictly smaller reciprocal-integer threshold and use relative closedness.</p><p>Failures of regularity or absence of ties are witnessed by one or two points in a common \\(C_m\\). For a tied pair impose separation at least \\(1/n\\), and test the values by the continuous expression in (19). Equality conditions and this separation condition have closed parameter projections because the source boxes are compact. Countably many \\(m,n\\) prove the other Borel tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a Borel assignment \\(R_F(z)\\), defined on \\(\\mathcal D\\), to a finite maximum over the same target:", "statement": "<p>**Strict measurable rank pairing (original Proposition 5.3).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal K,k,V_\\xi^F,G_F,J_F,\\mathcal G\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the Borel domain\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D=\n  \\{(F,z):J_F(z)&gt;0,\\ (F,G_F(z))\\in\\mathcal G\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a Borel assignment \\(R_F(z)\\), defined on \\(\\mathcal D\\), to a finite maximum over the same target:\n\\[\nG_F(R_F(z))=G_F(z),\n\\qquad\nJ_F(R_F(z))&lt;0.\n\\]\nIt is injective on each target fiber and satisfies\n\\[\nV_{G_F(z)}^F(R_F(z))\n&gt;\nV_{G_F(z)}^F(z).\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\nIt is equivariant:\n\\[\nR_F(t\\cdot w)=t\\cdot R_{T_tF}(w)\n\\]\non the corresponding domains.</p>", "proof": "<p>On each good fiber order saddles by decreasing critical value, and separately order finite maxima the same way. Each point has finitely many predecessors. A nonempty list has a first point: choose any one point and inspect the finite nonempty set at or above its value. Repeating gives integer ranks for all points.</p><p>Lemma 5.1 supplies at least as many maxima as saddles at or above each saddle’s value. Pair the saddle of rank \\(j\\) with the maximum of rank \\(j\\). No ties are allowed, so the paired value is strictly larger. Different ranks give different partners. Equation (22) preserves ranks, proving equivariance.</p><p>For measurability, use the nonsingularity of each root. The nonsingular solution graph\n\\[\nG_F(z)=\\xi,\\qquad J_F(z)\\ne0\n\\]\nhas countably many continuous local root branches \\(b_j(F,\\xi)\\), defined on open parameter sets.</p><p>To obtain a branch near \\((F_0,\\xi_0,z_0)\\), set \\(M=DG_{F_0}(z_0)\\). On a sufficiently small closed source ball,\n\\[\nw\\longmapsto w-M^{-1}(G_F(w)-\\xi)\n\\]\nhas Lipschitz constant at most \\(1/2\\) for all nearby parameters. Shrinking the parameter neighborhood makes the displacement of the center less than half the radius. The map is a contraction of the ball into itself, with a unique interior nonsingular root. Compactness and uniqueness imply continuous dependence on the parameters.</p><p>These branches form relatively open graph patches; second countability of \\(\\mathcal K\\times\\mathbb C\\times\\mathbb H\\) gives a countable cover.</p><p>At each parameter activate branch \\(j\\) only when it is defined and its value differs from every earlier defined branch value. This Borel test enumerates each root once. The signs of \\(J\\), critical values (19), and comparisons of such values are Borel. On good fibers, counting active predecessors of the same sign gives Borel integer ranks.</p><p>Selecting the unique active negative-sign branch of the same rank as a given active positive-sign branch is therefore Borel. Substitute \\(\\xi=G_F(z)\\) to obtain \\(R_F(z)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Both counts below are finite, and \\( \\#\\{z:z\\text{ is a finite maximum},\\ V_\\xi^F(z)\\ge h\\} \\ge \\#\\{z:z\\text{ is a saddle},\\ V_\\xi^F(z)\\ge h\\}. \\)", "statement": "<p>**The superlevel index count (original Lemma 5.2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((F,\\xi)\\in\\mathcal G\\) be a good pair.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both counts below are finite, and\n\\[\n\\#\\{z:z\\text{ is a finite maximum},\\ V_\\xi^F(z)\\ge h\\}\n\\ge\n\\#\\{z:z\\text{ is a saddle},\\ V_\\xi^F(z)\\ge h\\}.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The fiber \\(G_F^{-1}(\\xi)\\) is closed and discrete, and the closed positive superlevel is compact. This proves finiteness. There can be no accumulation at a pole either, because a pole is not on the fiber.</p><p>First count in\n\\[\nU=\\{V_\\xi^F&gt;\\lambda\\}\n\\]\nfor a positive regular level \\(\\lambda\\). Each component is bounded by one simple closed curve and has total gradient index one, giving\n\\[\n\\#\\text{components}\n=\n\\#\\text{finite maxima}-\\#\\text{saddles}+\\#\\text{poles}\n\\quad\\text{in }U.\n\\tag{24}\n\\]</p><p>To prove these assertions, the boundary of \\(U\\) is the compact smooth one-manifold \\(\\{V_\\xi^F=\\lambda\\}\\), away from any pole. It is a finite disjoint union of smooth simple closed curves. Its components are open in this compact manifold, so there are finitely many. Each component is one orbit of the smooth unit tangent field obtained by rotating \\(\\nabla\\ell/|\\nabla\\ell|\\), where \\(\\ell=\\log V_\\xi^F\\). The orbits are complete and open. A nonperiodic single orbit would be a bijective local homeomorphism from \\(\\mathbb R\\) to the compact component, which is impossible. Its least period gives a simple closed curve.</p><p>The bounded Jordan interior of each curve has closure in \\(\\mathbb H\\): the region below the curve’s minimum height lies in the unbounded complement. Everywhere inside, \\(V_\\xi^F&gt;\\lambda\\). Otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would have an interior minimum, contrary to\n\\[\n\\Delta\\ell=-k/y^2&lt;0.\n\\]\nIf a pole is present, remove a sufficiently small neighborhood where \\(\\ell\\) is large before taking this minimum.</p><p>Thus curves cannot be nested, and their interiors are exactly the components of \\(U\\). Indeed, each component meets the level boundary, has its superlevel side locally inside one of the curves, and cannot leave that interior without crossing the level.</p><p>On each positively oriented curve, \\(\\nabla\\ell\\) points inward and has winding one. Recall the tangent-turning argument of Hopf [Hop35, Section 2]. Parametrize a simple regular curve by \\(\\gamma:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb C\\), with matching endpoint derivatives, starting at a leftmost point. The positive tangent initially points down. The unit secant direction from \\(\\gamma(s)\\) to \\(\\gamma(t)\\), \\(0\\le s\\le t\\le1\\), extends continuously to the diagonal by the tangent, and to \\((0,1)\\) by the negative initial tangent.</p><p>Lift its angle continuously on this triangle. On \\((0,t)\\) it lies in the closed right semicircle and changes angle by \\(\\pi\\). On \\((s,1)\\) it is the negative of the direction at \\((0,s)\\), giving another \\(\\pi\\). The diagonal therefore records tangent winding one, unchanged for the inward normal.</p><p>Remove disjoint small disks about the finitely many critical points and possible pole in each component. Stokes applied to the closed angle form\n\\[\n\\frac{X_1\\,dX_2-X_2\\,dX_1}{|X|^2},\n\\qquad X=\\nabla\\ell,\n\\]\nshows that the sum of interior indices is one. At a nondegenerate critical point the index is the sign of the Hessian determinant, hence \\(+1\\) for a maximum and \\(-1\\) for a saddle. At the pole \\(z_0\\),\n\\[\n\\ell=-\\log|z-z_0|+\\text{a smooth function},\n\\]\ngiving index \\(+1\\). Summing over components proves (24).</p><p>There is at most one pole; if present, it belongs to a component. Thus the finite-maximum count is at least the saddle count. This also covers the empty superlevel.</p><p>Finally choose a regular \\(\\lambda&lt;h\\) with no critical value in \\([\\lambda,h)\\). Such a choice is possible by finiteness above \\(h/2\\). The counts in \\(U\\) are then the counts in (23).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\mathbb EJ_F(i)\\le0. \\) Equality is possible only if \\(J_F(i)=0\\) almost surely.", "statement": "<p>**Strict weighted transport (original Proposition 6.2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\beta\\ge1/4\\), let \\(P\\) satisfy (10), and put \\(k=(\\beta+2)/3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(G_F,J_F,V_\\xi^F,\\mathcal G\\) from Proposition 4 and \\(\\mathcal D,R_F\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  J_{F,+}=\\max(J_F,0),\n  \\qquad\n  J_{F,-}=\\max(-J_F,0),\n  \\qquad\n  \\omega_F(x+iy)=y^{\\beta-1}|Q_F(x+iy)|.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb EJ_F(i)\\le0.\n\\]\nEquality is possible only if \\(J_F(i)=0\\) almost surely.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(N\\ge1\\), restrict \\(\\mathcal D\\) to \\(\\mathcal D_N\\) by requiring, for\n\\[\nz=x+iy,\n\\qquad\nz'=R_F(z)=x'+iy',\n\\]\nthat\n\\[\n1/N\\le y'/y\\le N,\n\\qquad\n|x'-x|\\le Ny.\n\\]\nThese Borel sets increase to \\(\\mathcal D\\), and their indicators at \\((F,z)\\) equal their indicators at \\((T_zF,i)\\).</p><p>Define the equivariant Borel ratio\n\\[\nc_F(z)=\n\\left(\n\\frac{V_{G_F(z)}^F(R_F(z))}\n     {V_{G_F(z)}^F(z)}\n\\right)^3&gt;1\n\\quad\\text{on }\\mathcal D,\n\\]\nand set it to zero elsewhere.</p><p>For \\(S&gt;0\\), \\(Y&gt;1\\), use the boxes\n\\[\nB=\\{|x|\\le S,\\ 1\\le y\\le Y\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nB'=\\{|x|\\le S+NY,\\ 1/N\\le y\\le NY\\}.\n\\]\nEvery included saddle in \\(B\\) has its partner in \\(B'\\). On the target side of (26), multiplication by \\(c_F\\) makes its weight exactly the partner’s weight by (27). Fiberwise injectivity of the pairing therefore proves, for every \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\int_B\\omega_FJ_{F,+}c_F1_{\\mathcal D_N}(F,z)\\,dA(z)\n\\le\n\\int_{B'}\\omega_FJ_{F,-}\\,dA(z).\n\\]</p><p>Take expectations and apply (10) to the equivariant nonnegative tests. The resulting inequality is\n\\[\n2S\\log Y\\,\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\nJ_{F,+}(i)c_F(i)1_{\\mathcal D_N}(F,i)\n\\right]\n\\le\n2(S+NY)(\\log Y+2\\log N)\\,\n\\mathbb EJ_{F,-}(i).\n\\]\nHere \\(J_F(i)\\) is bounded by compact-family continuity, so the right side is finite.</p><p>At fixed \\(N\\), first let \\(S\\to\\infty\\), then \\(Y\\to\\infty\\). Finally let \\(N\\to\\infty\\) by monotone convergence. This gives\n\\[\nA:=\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\nJ_{F,+}(i)c_F(i)1_{\\mathcal D}(F,i)\n\\right]\n\\le\nd:=\\mathbb EJ_{F,-}(i).\n\\]\nLemma 6.2 gives\n\\[\nb:=\\mathbb EJ_{F,+}(i)\n=\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\nJ_{F,+}(i)1_{\\mathcal D}(F,i)\n\\right].\n\\]\nThus \\(b\\le A\\le d\\), proving \\(\\mathbb EJ_F(i)\\le0\\).</p><p>If equality holds, then \\(b=d\\) and\n\\[\n0\\le\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\nJ_{F,+}(i)(c_F(i)-1)1_{\\mathcal D}(F,i)\n\\right]\n=A-b\\le0.\n\\]\nThe integrand is strictly positive wherever \\(J_{F,+}(i)&gt;0\\) on \\(\\mathcal D\\). Equation (28) removes positive mass outside this domain, so \\(b=0\\), and hence \\(d=0\\). Therefore \\(J_F(i)=0\\) almost surely. No uniform positive gap in \\(c_F-1\\) is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For either sign, \\( \\int_{\\mathbb H}h(z)\\omega_F(z)J_{F,\\pm}(z)\\,dA(z) = \\int_{\\mathbb C} \\sum_{\\substack{G_F(z)=\\xi\\\\ \\pm J_F(z)>0}} h(z)y^{\\beta-1}|Q_F(z)|^3\\,dA(\\xi). \\) At every preimage \\(G_F(z)=\\xi\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Change of variables and critical-value weights.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(F\\in\\mathcal K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(G_F,J_{F,\\pm},\\omega_F,k\\) from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h:\\mathbb H\\to[0,\\infty]\\) be Borel.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For either sign,\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb H}h(z)\\omega_F(z)J_{F,\\pm}(z)\\,dA(z)\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb C}\n\\sum_{\\substack{G_F(z)=\\xi\\\\ \\pm J_F(z)&gt;0}}\nh(z)y^{\\beta-1}|Q_F(z)|^3\\,dA(\\xi).\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\nAt every preimage \\(G_F(z)=\\xi\\),\n\\[\ny^{\\beta-1}|Q_F(z)|^3\n=\\left(\\frac{V_\\xi^F(z)}k\\right)^3.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Cover the indicated sign locus by countably many local diffeomorphism charts, disjointify into Borel pieces, and use the absolute Jacobian\n\\[\n|\\det DG_F|=|J_F||F'|^2.\n\\]\nThis gives (26). No global properness of \\(G_F\\) is required.</p><p>At a preimage, (19) and \\(3(k-1)=\\beta-1\\) give (27).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The positive-Jacobian mass at the base point is supported on good fibers:", "statement": "<p>**Goodness at the normalized point (original Lemma 6.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the probability \\(P\\), Jacobian \\(J_F\\), critical map \\(G_F\\), and good-pair set \\(\\mathcal G\\) from Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The positive-Jacobian mass at the base point is supported on good fibers:\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\nJ_{F,+}(i)\\,\n1_{\\{(F,G_F(i))\\notin\\mathcal G\\}}\n\\right]=0.\n\\tag{28}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For almost every \\(F\\), bad targets have area zero by Proposition 4. Formula (26) therefore gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\{|x|\\le1,\\ 1\\le y\\le2\\}}\n\\omega_F(z)J_{F,+}(z)\n1_{\\{(F,G_F(z))\\notin\\mathcal G\\}}\\,dA(z)=0.\n\\]</p><p>The product of \\(J_{F,+}\\) and the indicator equals its base-point version for \\(T_zF\\), by (21)–(22). Take expectations and apply (10) and Tonelli. The spatial density is \\(dx\\,dy/y\\); its integral on this box is \\(2\\log2&gt;0\\). This proves (28).</p><p>The weighted assertion is essential: \\(G_F(i)=-i/k\\) for every \\(F\\), so area-genericity alone says nothing about this fixed target. Only the weighted conclusion is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "No \\(F\\in\\mathcal K\\) satisfies \\(J_F\\equiv0\\).", "statement": "<p>**The harmonic equality barrier (original Lemma 7.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal K=\\{F:\\mathbb H\\to\\mathbb C\\text{ univalent}:F(i)=0,\\ F'(i)=1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(k=3/4\\), and for \\(z=x+iy\\) define\n  \\[\n  q_F(z)=-iy\\frac{F''(z)}{F'(z)},\n  \\qquad\n  G_F(z)=F(z)-\\frac{iyF'(z)}k,\n  \\qquad\n  J_F(z)=\\frac{\\det DG_F(z)}{|F'(z)|^2}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>No \\(F\\in\\mathcal K\\) satisfies \\(J_F\\equiv0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>By (18), such a map would satisfy\n\\[\n|q_F(z)+1/4|^2=1/4.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n|q_F(z)|\\ge1/4,\n\\qquad\n\\left|\\frac{F''(z)}{F'(z)}\\right|\\ge\\frac1{4y}.\n\\]\nThe pre-Schwarzian has no zeros, so\n\\[\nu=\\log\\left|\\frac{F''}{F'}\\right|\n\\]\nis harmonic and\n\\[\nu\\ge-\\log4-\\log y.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1/2\\), the harmonic function\n\\[\nb_\\epsilon(x,y)\n=\n-\\log4+\n\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\cos(\\pi x/2)\n\\frac{\\sinh(\\pi(1-y)/2)}{\\sinh(\\pi/2)}\n\\]\nlies below \\(u\\) on the boundary of\n\\[\n[-1,1]\\times[\\epsilon,1].\n\\]\nOn its sides and top the added term is zero; on its bottom the cosine-sinh factor is between zero and one. The minimum principle gives \\(u\\ge b_\\epsilon\\) inside.</p><p>At \\(i/2\\), the coefficient of \\(\\log(1/\\epsilon)\\) is fixed and positive, contradicting finiteness as \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 6, "cite"], [1, 11, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [6, 11, "cite"], [6, 13, "cite"], [7, 2, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [8, 3, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [10, 15, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [12, 6, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [13, 20, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 13, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 18, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"]]});
